WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.920
RETO MEIER: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.550
hosted a plethora of talks
detailing the future of cloud.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.010
If you didn't get a chance to
catch Evan Anderson's session

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.260
on DevOps with App
Engine, stay tuned,

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.520
because here's the recap.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.795
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:17.920
COLT MCANLIS: When you've
got a DevOps system in place,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.240
the biggest enemy you have
is a concept called toil.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.980
RETO MEIER: Toil is
the work you do today

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.930
that you'll need to
do again tomorrow,

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.180
which is a problem,
because it scales linearly

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.500
with system growth.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.450
And it's work that
doesn't need much judgment

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.009
or creative problem-solving.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.404
COLT MCANLIS: Now, the
antidote to toil is automation.

00:00:34.404 --> 00:00:36.570
As you know, there's a lot
of data center automation

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.700
solutions out there already.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.340
Some, like a Chef,
Salt, and Puppet,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.100
refer to themselves
as fleet management,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.310
while others, like a Kubernetes,
Mesos, and Docker Engine,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.370
are typically called
container orchestration.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.400
Both sets of solutions do
effectively the same things.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.780
They help you manage and
deploy images and build

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:01.020
so that you don't go crazy.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.713
But there is another layer that
removes even more of your toil

00:01:03.713 --> 00:01:05.129
and lets you take
your application

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:06.600
management to the next level.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.330
And that, my friends,
is App Engine.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.630
But first, here is Dickerson's
hierarchy of reliability,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:16.500
created by the Googlers working
on the Healthcare.gov project.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.150
Each layer moves you
from things which

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.082
are required towards things that
are just really nice to have.

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:23.790
RETO MEIER: This shot
helps us understand

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.670
what we need in order to
properly provide reliability

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.620
to a service,
which, at its core,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.380
is the whole goal behind DevOps.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.414
This is where App
Engine fits in.

00:01:33.414 --> 00:01:34.830
When it comes to
all the tools you

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:38.130
need to provide reliability,
App Engine has you covered.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.220
Let's start with monitoring.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.480
Because App Engine is a
platform as a service,

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.970
all your top-level
metrics are already logged

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.180
and available-- things like
request latency and queue times

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.880
are automatic and can be
tracked across the application

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.820
as a whole, or at specific
URLs without needing

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.860
to write any application code.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.870
Just open Stackdriver,
and all this data

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.950
is exposed alongside monitoring
and inspection tools,

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.050
so you can export additional
metrics directly, or from logs,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.570
and align them with
your instance data.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.400
COLT MCANLIS: Next
is incident response,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.420
AKA how fast can we get back
to normal when things go wrong?

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.700
App Engine is designed
to automatically retain

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.090
previously deployed
versions and all audit logs.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.980
When something bad
happens, you can roll back

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.800
to an earlier deployment
in a single action.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.410
And App Engine will handle
all of the traffic splitting

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.710
and deployments to make
this happen seamlessly.

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.020
RETO MEIER: Then there's
testing and release.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.850
Chances are you don't want to
test everything in production.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:38.070
Accordingly, App Engine supports
multiple concurrent deployed

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.690
versions and gives
you control needed

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.840
to stage releases and balance
traffic between builds.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.760
Not to mention App
Engine has integrations

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.080
with a number of CI tools, like
Jenkins, Travis, and Circle CI.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.270
COLT MCANLIS: Which brings
us to capacity planning.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:54.690
App Engine makes capacity
planning magical.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.450
Automatic scaling,
including scale

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.470
to zero for unused
versions, reduces

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:02.040
the need for capacity
planning and any reservations.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.550
RETO MEIER: Finally, at the
very top of the pyramid is UX.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.380
This one is pretty
straightforward.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.500
Stackdriver covers a whole
suite of UX opportunities

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.570
to display your data and
monitoring information

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.740
in a bunch of different ways.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.740
And you can also build
your own dashboards

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.420
to get focused
insight on the numbers

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.190
that matter most to you.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.937
So make sure you follow the
hierarchy of reliability,

00:03:22.937 --> 00:03:25.020
and check out the recording
of Evan's full session

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.960
to see some practical examples
of using App Engine to stretch

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.910
your DevOps dollar further.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.260
If you want more recaps
on great Next content,

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.260
make sure to check out
the rest of our playlist,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.480
and don't forget the Next
world tour coming soon

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.960
to a city near you.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

